.TH MONITOR 3
.SH NAME
monitor \- prepare execution profile
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "monitor(lowpc, highpc, buffer, bufsize, nfunc)"
.B "int (*lowpc)( ), (*highpc)( );"
.B "short buffer[ ];"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An executable program created by
.B "`cc \-p'"
automatically includes calls
for
.B monitor
with default parameters;
.B monitor
needn't be called explicitly
except to gain fine control over profiling.
.PP
.B monitor
is an interface to
.BR profil (2).
.I lowpc
and
.I highpc
are the addresses
of two functions;
.I buffer
is the address of a (user supplied) array of
.I bufsize
short integers.
.B monitor
arranges to record a histogram of periodically
sampled values of the program counter, and of counts of calls of
certain functions, in the buffer.
The lowest address sampled is that of
.I lowpc
and the highest is just below
.IR highpc .
At most
.I nfunc
call counts
can be kept; only calls of functions compiled with the profiling option
.B \-p
of
.BR cc (1)
are recorded.
For the results to be significant, especially
where there are small, heavily used routines, it is suggested that the
buffer be no more than a few times smaller than the range of locations
sampled.
.PP
To profile the entire program, it is sufficient to use
.PP
.RS
.nf
extern etext( );
\&...
monitor((int)2, etext, buf, bufsize, nfunc);
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B etext
lies just above all the program text, see
.BR end (3).
.PP
To stop execution monitoring and write the results on the file
.BR mon.out ,
use
.PP
.RS
monitor(0);
.RE
.PP
then
.BR prof (1)
can be used to examine the results.
.SH FILES
mon.out
.SH "SEE ALSO"
prof(1), profil(2), cc(1)
.sp
